Revision history for Net-Async-Kubernetes

{{$NEXT}}
 - Add public `rest` and `new_object` methods on the client. `rest` exposes the
   underlying `Kubernetes::REST` instance and `new_object($kind, %fields)`
   builds a typed `IO::K8s` object for `create`/`update`, so callers no longer
   reach through the private `_rest` accessor. `_rest` stays as the private
   internal accessor and returns the same cached instance as `rest`.
 - Require `IO::K8s` 1.108 and `Kubernetes::REST` 1.108 -- the two floors this
   release actually ships; the entries further down record the intermediate
   steps that led there. Anything below `IO::K8s` 1.108 resolves a bare Kind
   name such as `Role` to whatever top-level package of that name is loadable
   -- `Class::Mite` ships a `lib/Role.pm`, for example -- so that package won
   the lookup before the resource map was ever consulted, and creating the
   object died with `Can't locate object method "_k8s_attr_info" via package
   "Role"` (GitHub issue #2, only ever on smokers carrying such a module).
   1.108 reads a bare one-word name as a Kind, so the resource map wins.
   `Event`, `Job`, `Service`, `Binding` and `Scale` share the collision.
   1.108 also carries the upstream Kubernetes v1.37 sync -- 22 new Kinds
   (among them the `IO::K8s::Api::Lifecycle` namespace), their supporting
   types and new fields, all registered for GVK dispatch. The client resolves
   them transitively through the `Kubernetes::REST` `expand_class` / inflate
   seam, so watching, listing and CRUD reach the new resources with no change
   here. `Kubernetes::REST` 1.108 is required alongside it because that is the
   release requiring `IO::K8s` 1.108 in turn: every Kind this client resolves
   goes through the `Kubernetes::REST` seam and the `IO::K8s` inside it, so
   pinning only one of the pair would leave the resolution that the GitHub
   issue #2 fix depends on to whatever version happened to sit underneath.
 - Move `Net::Async::Kubernetes::PortForwardSession` into its own file. The
   class was declared inline at the bottom of `Net::Async::Kubernetes`, so the
   provides map pointed at a file that does not carry its name and every
   `dzil build` warned it may not be loadable at install time -- and indeed
   `use Net::Async::Kubernetes::PortForwardSession` failed for anyone loading
   the class the POD of `port_forward()`, `exec()` and `attach()` names. The
   code is unchanged and the client loads it at compile time.
 - Add `on_watch_error` to `Net::Async::Kubernetes::Controller`. Watch `ERROR`
   events (a `403` arriving mid-stream, for example) were wired nowhere and
   stayed invisible to the controller. They carry a `Status` hashref rather
   than a keyed resource object, so they are reported to the callback instead
   of entering the reconcile workqueue. An `on_error` passed to
   `watch_resource` keeps precedence for that watch.
 - Fix a reference cycle that leaked the client bound to a controller. The
   controller held a strong reference to `kube` while `$kube->controller(...)`
   registers the controller as a child, so neither was ever freed. A supplied
   client is now weakened, as in `Net::Async::Kubernetes::Watcher`; a client
   the controller constructs itself stays owned by it.
 - Fix the controller keeping one workqueue entry per reconcile key for the
   lifetime of the process. Nothing ever removed an entry, so a controller on
   a high churn resource grew its keyspace without bound, each entry pinning
   the last object it saw. A key is now dropped once it reconciles cleanly --
   a `DELETED` event needs no case of its own, its reconcile ends the same
   way. Keys that are still queued, dirty or waiting on a retry keep their
   entry, so backoff history is never lost and the next failure does not
   restart at attempt 1.
 - Fix a second reference cycle in the controller, this one closed on itself:
   the reconcile context stored in an entry held a strong `controller`, and
   the entry lives in the controller. Any entry outliving its reconcile --
   a failed key, a pending retry, anything still queued at shutdown -- leaked
   the controller with no outside reference involved. `controller` in the
   context is now weak, like `kube`. It stays valid for the whole reconcile;
   a context kept past that no longer keeps the controller alive.
 - Fix the duplex transport reporting `port_forward` in its "not added to an
   IO::Async::Loop" failure regardless of which method was called; `exec()`
   and `attach()` now name themselves.
 - Fix a resource name that `IO::K8s` cannot resolve killing the caller
   instead of failing its `Future`. `expand_class` fails closed -- an
   unknown, malformed or mismatched apiVersion resolves to undef rather than
   a bare-name guess -- and that undef went straight into `build_path`, which
   died with "argument is not a module name", naming neither the resource nor
   the reason. `list()`, `get()`, `patch()`, `delete()`, `log()`,
   `port_forward()`, `exec()`, `attach()` and the controller's
   `patch_status()` now return a failed `Future` naming the resource, keeping
   the contract their callers await on. The two genuinely synchronous paths,
   `$kube->expand_class` and starting a watcher, croak with the same message.
 - Fix a controller key stalling after a stop/start cycle. `stop()` cleared the
   retry timers but left the workqueue filled and the keys on it flagged as
   queued, so the event a restarted watch delivers for such a key was
   deduplicated away without ever scheduling a drain -- the key then waited for
   an unrelated key to drag it out of the queue. `stop()` now clears the queue
   and those flags together: a restart re-lists and delivers everything still
   present anyway, and the failure counts stay behind, so a retrying key still
   resumes at its next attempt number.
 - Fix the reconcile drain abandoning the whole queue when it meets a queued
   key whose entry is gone, instead of skipping that one key. The state is not
   reachable today -- an entry is only dropped while its key is not queued --
   but it made loosening that condition a silent stall for every key behind it.
 - Fix that same stall returning through the requeue of a dirty key. An event
   arriving while its key reconciles is recorded as dirty instead of queued,
   and the requeue once that reconcile finished pushed the key back without
   checking whether the controller had stopped meanwhile -- refilling the
   queue `stop()` had just emptied, with no drain scheduled to take it out
   again. The requeue now skips while stopped, like every other place that
   feeds the queue, and `stop()` clears the dirty flag along with the queued
   one: it is pending work too, and the restart re-lists that object anyway.
 - Fix `stop()` leaving a watcher attached to the client on every stop/start
   cycle. `$kube->watcher(...)` registers the watcher as a child of the client,
   and `stop()` stopped it and dropped it from the watch spec without the
   matching removal, while `start()` built a fresh one -- so a repeatedly
   restarted controller piled up stopped watcher notifiers on the client, all
   of them still reachable. A stopped watch is now detached as well.
 - Require `Kubernetes::REST` 1.106 (was 1.102) and `IO::K8s` 1.105 (was
   1.008). Kubernetes::REST 1.106 encodes request bodies as UTF-8 bytes;
   every `create`, `update` and `patch` here builds its body through
   `prepare_request`, so before that fix a manifest carrying a non-ASCII
   character died with "HTTP::Message content must be bytes" without ever
   reaching the cluster. That release requires IO::K8s 1.105 in turn.
 - Require `IO::K8s` 1.106 (was 1.105) for two fixes that land on seams this
   client uses on every request. `Kubernetes::REST` derives every URL path
   from `$class->api_version`, and for the Storagemigration and
   Apiserverinternal groups 1.105 returned a lowercased class-name fragment --
   so `list()`, `get()` and every writing method addressed
   `/apis/storagemigration/v1alpha1` and `/apis/apiserverinternal/v1alpha1`,
   neither of which the API server serves. 1.106 returns the upstream group
   names. The short names were no better off: they resolved to a bogus
   `IO::K8s::StorageVersionMigration`, which is not undef, so the
   fail-closed guard above let it through to `build_path` and its raw
   "Can't locate ... in @INC" -- exactly the error that guard exists to
   replace.
 - The second fix is `_expand_class`, which could not match a CamelCase
   namespace prefix and sent KubeAggregator's `APIService` looking for its
   spec and status under `IO::K8s::Api::KubeAggregator::V1`. Because each of
   this client's inflation paths handles a failure differently, one bug had
   three faces: `get()` died on the response, `list()` dropped every item
   inside its per-item `eval` and returned an empty `IO::K8s::List`, and a
   watch handed `on_added` the raw hashref instead of a typed object.
 - Gain a capability with the same release: its exact GVK dispatch (113
   qualified `$apiVersion/$Kind` map keys, previously none) means a resource
   name of the form `group/version/Kind` now resolves anywhere this client
   takes one. `list('autoscaling/v1/HorizontalPodAutoscaler')` addresses
   `/apis/autoscaling/v1/...`, while the bare `HorizontalPodAutoscaler` keeps
   reaching the shipped default of v2 -- so a caller can finally target a
   version other than the default. Under 1.105 the qualified form resolved to
   undef and failed.
 - Stop `t/03-configure.t` reading the kubeconfig of the machine it runs on.
   The subtest guarded only on the file existing and then loaded
   `$ENV{KUBECONFIG} // "$ENV{HOME}/.kube/config"`, so the result depended on
   the smoker: every report for 0.005 from one CPAN Testers machine was a FAIL
   and nobody else saw it, because that machine carried a kubeconfig with
   `contexts: null` and an empty `current-context`. Resolution croaked, the
   subtest died before running a single test, and the file exited 255. A valid
   foreign kubeconfig is no better a basis -- an exec auth plugin or an
   unreachable cluster fails just as readily. The subtest now runs against a
   fixture written to a temporary HOME, so it neither skips nor depends on the
   machine, and a companion subtest holds the degenerate config to a catchable
   croak instead of a dead run. Reported by Andreas Koenig, GitHub issue #1.
 - Load `Net::Async::Kubernetes::Watcher` and
   `Net::Async::Kubernetes::Controller` at compile time instead of `require`ing
   them inside `watcher()` and `controller()`. Nothing changes for a caller
   except when a child module is broken: that now surfaces on
   `use Net::Async::Kubernetes` rather than on the first `->watcher()` call.
   There is no load cycle to avoid -- neither child touches the client at
   compile time.
 - Move the example scripts from `ex/` to `eg/` and correct the paths naming
   them. The 0.007 entry below still calls the live-feature demo
   `ex/live_features.pl`; it ships as `eg/live_features.pl`, alongside
   `eg/demo.pl` and `eg/watch_pods.pl`.
 - Documentation pass over the POD: the hand-written `NAME` sections are gone
   (PodWeaver generates them), `Net::Async::Kubernetes::PortForwardSession` is
   documented and reachable from `SEE ALSO`, `list()` and the `cp` helpers
   describe what they actually do, the qualified `group/version/Kind` resource
   name and the status-subresource write-strip trap are written down, and the
   `Controller` methods state the `Future` they return and what it resolves to.

0.007     2026-04-15 18:21:50Z
 - Add `Net::Async::Kubernetes::Controller` as a minimal controller runtime.
   Provides watch registration, keyed workqueue deduplication, serialized
   reconcile dispatch, retry hooks, and small helpers for object reload and
   status subresource patch/update.
 - Add `$kube->controller(...)` factory to bind controller runtimes directly
   to an existing async Kubernetes client.
 - Fix async TLS handling for kubeconfigs with inline CA/client PEM data by
   materializing PEM content to temporary files before passing SSL options to
   Net::Async::HTTP / IO::Socket::SSL. This unblocks mTLS-authenticated async
   clients and controller runtimes against real clusters using embedded certs.
 - Add active `exec()` API that builds Kubernetes pod exec requests
   (`/exec` subresource) and delegates to the websocket duplex transport.
   Supports command, container, stdin/stdout/stderr/tty toggles, and
   subprotocol override.
 - Add active `attach()` API that builds Kubernetes pod attach requests
   (`/attach` subresource) and delegates to the websocket duplex transport.
   Supports container, stdin/stdout/stderr/tty toggles, and subprotocol
   override.
 - Extend `Net::Async::Kubernetes::PortForwardSession` with `write_stdin`
   (writes to channel 0) and `resize` (writes TTY resize JSON to channel 4)
   helpers for exec/attach sessions.
 - Add async `cp_to_pod()` and `cp_from_pod()` helpers that stream tar
   archives through an exec session for file transfer.
 - Add mock test coverage for exec (`t/14-mock-exec.t`), attach
   (`t/15-mock-attach.t`), and cp (`t/16-mock-cp.t`) flows.

0.006     2026-03-09 08:37:54Z
 - Add async Pod Log API via `log()`.
   Supports one-shot mode (Future resolves to full text) and streaming mode
   (`on_line` callback with Kubernetes::REST::LogEvent objects).
   Streaming mode supports follow, tailLines, sinceSeconds, sinceTime,
   timestamps, previous, limitBytes, and container parameters.
 - Add mock log test coverage (`t/09-mock-log.t`) and extend mock transport
   with generic streaming chunk support for log streams.
 - Add active `port_forward()` API that builds Kubernetes port-forward requests
   and delegates to duplex transport (`_do_duplex_request`).
 - Implement default websocket duplex transport for `port_forward()` using
   Net::Async::WebSocket::Client, including channel frame decoding and a
   `Net::Async::Kubernetes::PortForwardSession` helper (`write_channel`,
   `close`).
 - Add duplex transport tests (`t/13-duplex-transport.t`) for websocket
   handshake wiring, frame handling, session writes/closes, and connect errors.
 - Add active `exec()` API that builds Kubernetes pod `/exec` requests with
   repeated `command=` parameters and stream toggles
   (stdin/stdout/stderr/tty).
 - Add async exec test coverage (`t/14-mock-exec.t`) and duplex transport
   assertions for exec in `t/13-duplex-transport.t`.
 - Add active `attach()` API that builds Kubernetes pod `/attach` requests with
   stream toggles (stdin/stdout/stderr/tty).
 - Add async attach test coverage (`t/15-mock-attach.t`) and duplex transport
   assertions for attach in `t/13-duplex-transport.t`.
 - Extend `Net::Async::Kubernetes::PortForwardSession` with convenience
   helpers `write_stdin()` (channel 0) and `resize()` (channel 4 terminal
   resize payload for exec/attach).
 - Add async cp helpers `cp_to_pod()` and `cp_from_pod()` built on `exec()`.
   Supports single-file upload/download via channel streaming with Future-based
   completion and status/error propagation.
 - Add cp helper mock tests in `t/16-mock-cp.t`.
 - Add live demo script `ex/live_features.pl` to exercise log, exec, attach,
   port-forward, and cp helpers against a real cluster/kubeconfig.
 - Raise minimum versions to Kubernetes::REST 1.101 and IO::K8s 1.008.

0.005     2026-03-04 17:14:31Z
 - Use public Kubernetes::REST building-block API (build_path,
   prepare_request, check_response, inflate_object, inflate_list,
   process_watch_chunk) instead of private underscore methods.
   Requires Kubernetes::REST >= 1.100.

0.004     2026-02-28 01:39:32Z

0.003     2026-02-28 01:11:24Z
 - Auto-detect kubeconfig and in-cluster service account via
   Kubernetes::REST::Kubeconfig (requires Kubernetes::REST >= 1.004).
   When no explicit server/kubeconfig is provided, construction silently
   tries default kubeconfig and in-cluster auth before falling back to
   lazy resolution.

0.003     2026-02-28 00:00:00Z

0.002     2026-02-18 22:52:19Z
 - Updating minimum requirements

0.001     2026-02-13 06:03:10Z
 - Initial release
 - Async Kubernetes client built on IO::Async and Kubernetes::REST
 - Future-based CRUD: list(), get(), create(), update(), patch(), delete()
 - Three patch types: strategic-merge (default), merge, json
 - Delete by class+name or by object reference
 - Net::Async::Kubernetes::Watcher for streaming watch with auto-reconnect
 - Separate on_added, on_modified, on_deleted, on_error callbacks
 - Catch-all on_event callback for raw WatchEvent access
 - Client-side event filtering: names (regex/string/array) and event_types
 - Smart event type auto-derivation from registered callbacks
 - Resumable watches via resourceVersion tracking
 - Automatic 410 Gone recovery (clear resourceVersion and restart)
 - Auto-reconnect on stream completion (server timeout) and connection errors
 - Kubeconfig support via Kubernetes::REST::Kubeconfig
 - SSL/TLS with client certificate support
 - Custom resource_map for CRD support
 - Pass Content-Type correctly to Net::Async::HTTP for all request bodies
 - Defer watcher stop() HTTP close to avoid Net::Async::HTTP spurious read errors
 - Comprehensive inline POD documentation for all public methods and attributes
